ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f9e1c226-db34-51b8-9ead-12e302e7f63a" :step/id #uuid "f9e1c226-db34-51b8-9ead-12e302e7f63a" :step/content "assigned-problem-d35884e6-1669-5364-a41a-a52efb1220e4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157276 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f89c3382-117b-502e-8af6-ebca79b6f083"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351219 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157277 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4d4f28f0-6e57-5f1c-b187-80e59b881c47" "question-fbba5a71-4786-5f34-9118-2344153343aa" "question-45f13db9-2e9d-5ea4-a615-95b4e61aba9f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d4f28f0-6e57-5f1c-b187-80e59b881c47" :question/id #uuid "4d4f28f0-6e57-5f1c-b187-80e59b881c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-81108" :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $2{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{41} \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{41} \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-15+0-8 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-23)=-46.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81c27db6-a846-5a45-83a3-5a70d18bec75"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81c27db6-a846-5a45-83a3-5a70d18bec75" :answer-field/id #uuid "81c27db6-a846-5a45-83a3-5a70d18bec75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b6f0d473-5189-54a8-aa7b-ef8f87c0b58c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5620e6f6-75a0-5f4a-8f11-1f0be3b41912" "answer-b6f0d473-5189-54a8-aa7b-ef8f87c0b58c" "answer-2323e7de-6036-569e-953d-d977998ad9b4" "answer-2297f5c1-de21-5ae7-bdaa-c11eef2db9ad" "answer-98d031cb-9e96-593d-bd25-1d64d893572e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5620e6f6-75a0-5f4a-8f11-1f0be3b41912" :answer/id #uuid "5620e6f6-75a0-5f4a-8f11-1f0be3b41912" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6f0d473-5189-54a8-aa7b-ef8f87c0b58c" :answer/id #uuid "b6f0d473-5189-54a8-aa7b-ef8f87c0b58c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-46" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2323e7de-6036-569e-953d-d977998ad9b4" :answer/id #uuid "2323e7de-6036-569e-953d-d977998ad9b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2297f5c1-de21-5ae7-bdaa-c11eef2db9ad" :answer/id #uuid "2297f5c1-de21-5ae7-bdaa-c11eef2db9ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98d031cb-9e96-593d-bd25-1d64d893572e" :answer/id #uuid "98d031cb-9e96-593d-bd25-1d64d893572e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fbba5a71-4786-5f34-9118-2344153343aa" :question/id #uuid "fbba5a71-4786-5f34-9118-2344153343aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-81140" :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}=3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f22f005-276f-5d7e-8ec0-5d5973524d87"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f22f005-276f-5d7e-8ec0-5d5973524d87" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f22f005-276f-5d7e-8ec0-5d5973524d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35584b0d-5f36-530c-95c4-8d8dec57a042" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b077d275-d97e-5b1d-b7c1-156af5d3749c" "answer-35584b0d-5f36-530c-95c4-8d8dec57a042" "answer-a7dfba1c-89fc-5799-a7ce-902a9097fb41" "answer-cf87e655-37bd-5819-8214-e0383ae42a09" "answer-62645a61-af8c-50c1-891a-ec2a1bd6ffa4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b077d275-d97e-5b1d-b7c1-156af5d3749c" :answer/id #uuid "b077d275-d97e-5b1d-b7c1-156af5d3749c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35584b0d-5f36-530c-95c4-8d8dec57a042" :answer/id #uuid "35584b0d-5f36-530c-95c4-8d8dec57a042" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7dfba1c-89fc-5799-a7ce-902a9097fb41" :answer/id #uuid "a7dfba1c-89fc-5799-a7ce-902a9097fb41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf87e655-37bd-5819-8214-e0383ae42a09" :answer/id #uuid "cf87e655-37bd-5819-8214-e0383ae42a09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62645a61-af8c-50c1-891a-ec2a1bd6ffa4" :answer/id #uuid "62645a61-af8c-50c1-891a-ec2a1bd6ffa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-45f13db9-2e9d-5ea4-a615-95b4e61aba9f" :question/id #uuid "45f13db9-2e9d-5ea4-a615-95b4e61aba9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-102324" :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=-12+8=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=16+6=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32a6cdb5-6723-5485-bdd3-27cef7d40f0f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32a6cdb5-6723-5485-bdd3-27cef7d40f0f" :answer-field/id #uuid "32a6cdb5-6723-5485-bdd3-27cef7d40f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3cf6c244-2a79-5865-8cb4-cbb552d3e654" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03308ccf-77b0-5a02-af96-d39b1048daf6" "answer-6c09e389-b27b-544f-b450-ec29c5574df9" "answer-e7ab850f-14bf-53dd-88ed-cd3f163a5e8f" "answer-3cf6c244-2a79-5865-8cb4-cbb552d3e654" "answer-83da0c7d-97f5-59a5-8e85-e915619fc0ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03308ccf-77b0-5a02-af96-d39b1048daf6" :answer/id #uuid "03308ccf-77b0-5a02-af96-d39b1048daf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c09e389-b27b-544f-b450-ec29c5574df9" :answer/id #uuid "6c09e389-b27b-544f-b450-ec29c5574df9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7ab850f-14bf-53dd-88ed-cd3f163a5e8f" :answer/id #uuid "e7ab850f-14bf-53dd-88ed-cd3f163a5e8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cf6c244-2a79-5865-8cb4-cbb552d3e654" :answer/id #uuid "3cf6c244-2a79-5865-8cb4-cbb552d3e654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83da0c7d-97f5-59a5-8e85-e915619fc0ff" :answer/id #uuid "83da0c7d-97f5-59a5-8e85-e915619fc0ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f89c3382-117b-502e-8af6-ebca79b6f083" :question/id #uuid "f89c3382-117b-502e-8af6-ebca79b6f083" :question/math-academy-id "q-102354" :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+8}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 3=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-846614c1-9a14-59e2-9c4d-0efd9f8946b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-846614c1-9a14-59e2-9c4d-0efd9f8946b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "846614c1-9a14-59e2-9c4d-0efd9f8946b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d5cedc30-2694-5244-bfcb-f9f82cdcc31e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89245d79-d07d-52fc-b200-d036359ea5dd" "answer-cbb2be99-4d40-5ce0-8615-04e936d37155" "answer-d5cedc30-2694-5244-bfcb-f9f82cdcc31e" "answer-ede240b8-195a-5dc0-9427-2e395b73f909" "answer-8ec329dc-1748-52dd-b167-d53c2b2de5f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89245d79-d07d-52fc-b200-d036359ea5dd" :answer/id #uuid "89245d79-d07d-52fc-b200-d036359ea5dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbb2be99-4d40-5ce0-8615-04e936d37155" :answer/id #uuid "cbb2be99-4d40-5ce0-8615-04e936d37155" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5cedc30-2694-5244-bfcb-f9f82cdcc31e" :answer/id #uuid "d5cedc30-2694-5244-bfcb-f9f82cdcc31e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ede240b8-195a-5dc0-9427-2e395b73f909" :answer/id #uuid "ede240b8-195a-5dc0-9427-2e395b73f909" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ec329dc-1748-52dd-b167-d53c2b2de5f8" :answer/id #uuid "8ec329dc-1748-52dd-b167-d53c2b2de5f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-cc22b251-df3f-5cc0-b38c-623425ac9228" :activity/id #uuid "cc22b251-df3f-5cc0-b38c-623425ac9228" :activity/title "The Components of a Vector with Respect to an Orthogonal or Orthonormal Basis" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050366 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-dd349e63-c92c-5105-b629-eece45df73c7" "step-b94c0da3-b4f4-5f78-9cf1-261b170244ad" "step-95863f60-8f06-563b-88de-fe49aa124204" "step-ece727ba-a6ce-5bd3-b189-44423b34df89" "step-42789cd5-e071-545e-b036-41b4b0e1042f"] :activity/first-step "step-dd349e63-c92c-5105-b629-eece45df73c7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-dd349e63-c92c-5105-b629-eece45df73c7" :step/id #uuid "dd349e63-c92c-5105-b629-eece45df73c7" :step/content 17592186351219 :step/next "step-b94c0da3-b4f4-5f78-9cf1-261b170244ad" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b94c0da3-b4f4-5f78-9cf1-261b170244ad" :step/id #uuid "b94c0da3-b4f4-5f78-9cf1-261b170244ad" :step/content "question-4d4f28f0-6e57-5f1c-b187-80e59b881c47" :step/next "step-95863f60-8f06-563b-88de-fe49aa124204" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-95863f60-8f06-563b-88de-fe49aa124204" :step/id #uuid "95863f60-8f06-563b-88de-fe49aa124204" :step/content "question-fbba5a71-4786-5f34-9118-2344153343aa" :step/next "step-ece727ba-a6ce-5bd3-b189-44423b34df89" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ece727ba-a6ce-5bd3-b189-44423b34df89" :step/id #uuid "ece727ba-a6ce-5bd3-b189-44423b34df89" :step/content "question-45f13db9-2e9d-5ea4-a615-95b4e61aba9f" :step/next "step-42789cd5-e071-545e-b036-41b4b0e1042f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-42789cd5-e071-545e-b036-41b4b0e1042f" :step/id #uuid "42789cd5-e071-545e-b036-41b4b0e1042f" :step/content "question-f89c3382-117b-502e-8af6-ebca79b6f083" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186178221 :answer-field/choices 17592186178224] [:db/retract 17592186178221 :answer-field/choices 17592186178225] [:db/retract 17592186178221 :answer-field/choices 17592186178226] [:db/retract 17592186178221 :answer-field/choices 17592186178222] [:db/retract 17592186178221 :answer-field/choices 17592186178223] [:db/retract 17592186178179 :answer-field/choices 17592186178181] [:db/retract 17592186178179 :answer-field/choices 17592186178182] [:db/retract 17592186178179 :answer-field/choices 17592186178183] [:db/retract 17592186178179 :answer-field/choices 17592186178184] [:db/retract 17592186177867 :answer-field/choices 17592186177868] [:db/retract 17592186177867 :answer-field/choices 17592186177869] [:db/retract 17592186177867 :answer-field/choices 17592186177870] [:db/retract 17592186177867 :answer-field/choices 17592186177871] [:db/retract 17592186180091 :question/answer-fields 17592186178215] {:db/id 17592186179765 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cae20d6b-2ae5-5a13-a8c2-7e9cf70b768d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179766 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9691dde-e78b-59c1-ac5c-550cb0f124bb" "question-e0611f00-dc8b-5374-b5f7-8b0d695e2534"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179427 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = x^4y\\,\\mathbf{i} - 2x^3y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\sin x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4x^2y\\,\\mathbf{i} -4x^2y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179774 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03021383-b99c-5e38-823a-268b5d1215a6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cae20d6b-2ae5-5a13-a8c2-7e9cf70b768d" :question/id #uuid "cae20d6b-2ae5-5a13-a8c2-7e9cf70b768d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162510" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{1-y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a4ad95fd-3ba5-57ea-89a2-4cf27fe1615b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a4ad95fd-3ba5-57ea-89a2-4cf27fe1615b" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ad95fd-3ba5-57ea-89a2-4cf27fe1615b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84a166b1-efc2-58de-b99b-61ca8760b1b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bef70330-ce0a-50b9-8bbf-129455a0ace1" "answer-79ab0051-8a1d-5b87-9e8b-c2a2a8676b53" "answer-470802a2-bab1-557b-aff1-7439468a51fb" "answer-0324db79-c1eb-5ed2-9131-3173b26268b3" "answer-84a166b1-efc2-58de-b99b-61ca8760b1b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bef70330-ce0a-50b9-8bbf-129455a0ace1" :answer/id #uuid "bef70330-ce0a-50b9-8bbf-129455a0ace1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=-2{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{-y}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79ab0051-8a1d-5b87-9e8b-c2a2a8676b53" :answer/id #uuid "79ab0051-8a1d-5b87-9e8b-c2a2a8676b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=-x{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2y{e}^{1-y}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-470802a2-bab1-557b-aff1-7439468a51fb" :answer/id #uuid "470802a2-bab1-557b-aff1-7439468a51fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=-x{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0324db79-c1eb-5ed2-9131-3173b26268b3" :answer/id #uuid "0324db79-c1eb-5ed2-9131-3173b26268b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=-{e}^{1-y}\\,i-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84a166b1-efc2-58de-b99b-61ca8760b1b4" :answer/id #uuid "84a166b1-efc2-58de-b99b-61ca8760b1b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{1-y}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180092 :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}x{e}^{xy} \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}(2+yx), \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186178221 :answer-field/correct "answer-1c07575d-294c-5293-b24e-6a9417e0599e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e4b5532-5cfc-569d-94b2-251c025302d2" "answer-1c3baa68-a9a3-512e-ac2c-44e118883d68" "answer-20503e7d-e6bc-5f3b-867f-11160df1589b" "answer-c9a03243-e451-5382-82f5-0ae156239adb" "answer-1c07575d-294c-5293-b24e-6a9417e0599e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e4b5532-5cfc-569d-94b2-251c025302d2" :answer/id #uuid "3e4b5532-5cfc-569d-94b2-251c025302d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}(2+y)\\,i-({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c3baa68-a9a3-512e-ac2c-44e118883d68" :answer/id #uuid "1c3baa68-a9a3-512e-ac2c-44e118883d68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-(yx{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20503e7d-e6bc-5f3b-867f-11160df1589b" :answer/id #uuid "20503e7d-e6bc-5f3b-867f-11160df1589b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=yx{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{2}x{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9a03243-e451-5382-82f5-0ae156239adb" :answer/id #uuid "c9a03243-e451-5382-82f5-0ae156239adb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+y)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c07575d-294c-5293-b24e-6a9417e0599e" :answer/id #uuid "1c07575d-294c-5293-b24e-6a9417e0599e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9691dde-e78b-59c1-ac5c-550cb0f124bb" :question/id #uuid "e9691dde-e78b-59c1-ac5c-550cb0f124bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162614" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}-2xy)\\,i+({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,i-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(2x-2y)+(2y-2x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)+(-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8d3f67a-8384-5a77-a9a4-eb3e9f68a0eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8d3f67a-8384-5a77-a9a4-eb3e9f68a0eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d3f67a-8384-5a77-a9a4-eb3e9f68a0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b29da8c3-1cc1-5e25-86bf-88ebbd6119ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc05f566-bd10-5158-8fb4-fe5ab7bbec72" "answer-95bce84c-a559-591f-953f-651e5baec02d" "answer-b29da8c3-1cc1-5e25-86bf-88ebbd6119ce" "answer-5778857a-821a-5682-89ba-1adfcab7df7c" "answer-bb5c5ded-90a1-5823-bb01-ab3f81ba4a0c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc05f566-bd10-5158-8fb4-fe5ab7bbec72" :answer/id #uuid "bc05f566-bd10-5158-8fb4-fe5ab7bbec72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95bce84c-a559-591f-953f-651e5baec02d" :answer/id #uuid "95bce84c-a559-591f-953f-651e5baec02d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b29da8c3-1cc1-5e25-86bf-88ebbd6119ce" :answer/id #uuid "b29da8c3-1cc1-5e25-86bf-88ebbd6119ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5778857a-821a-5682-89ba-1adfcab7df7c" :answer/id #uuid "5778857a-821a-5682-89ba-1adfcab7df7c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb5c5ded-90a1-5823-bb01-ab3f81ba4a0c" :answer/id #uuid "bb5c5ded-90a1-5823-bb01-ab3f81ba4a0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180093 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=4xy\\,i+{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,i+3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)={e}^{xy}\\,i-y{e}^{x}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4y+4{y}^{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y{e}^{xy}+(-{e}^{x})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186178227 :answer-field/correct 17592186178230 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0611f00-dc8b-5374-b5f7-8b0d695e2534" :question/id #uuid "e0611f00-dc8b-5374-b5f7-8b0d695e2534" :question/math-academy-id "q-162612" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=x\\,i+2{e}^{y}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+2{e}^{y}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72ba71ce-b94a-5573-a9f5-03870c73d863"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72ba71ce-b94a-5573-a9f5-03870c73d863" :answer-field/id #uuid "72ba71ce-b94a-5573-a9f5-03870c73d863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-335a35ea-9c7e-52ad-891b-16a96af5a8c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9cca9909-11f3-54e5-be7a-416185202e4f" "answer-b6ed420d-9af0-5de8-8f51-6a8eb4d4d2a3" "answer-25c55a7f-31b3-58c2-b77e-afb1cd059f2b" "answer-335a35ea-9c7e-52ad-891b-16a96af5a8c5" "answer-bdf91090-4a1d-5328-8dcd-f3143efe1729"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cca9909-11f3-54e5-be7a-416185202e4f" :answer/id #uuid "9cca9909-11f3-54e5-be7a-416185202e4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6ed420d-9af0-5de8-8f51-6a8eb4d4d2a3" :answer/id #uuid "b6ed420d-9af0-5de8-8f51-6a8eb4d4d2a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25c55a7f-31b3-58c2-b77e-afb1cd059f2b" :answer/id #uuid "25c55a7f-31b3-58c2-b77e-afb1cd059f2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-335a35ea-9c7e-52ad-891b-16a96af5a8c5" :answer/id #uuid "335a35ea-9c7e-52ad-891b-16a96af5a8c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdf91090-4a1d-5328-8dcd-f3143efe1729" :answer/id #uuid "bdf91090-4a1d-5328-8dcd-f3143efe1729" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180085 :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=1-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-2y+(-2y)=-4y\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186178179 :answer-field/correct 17592186178180 :answer-field/choices ["answer-430fd24f-3207-570e-9148-bac816a3c0be" "answer-1060863a-42e6-5905-845d-98d31c4eb37b" "answer-29b072d1-5990-5d33-8af7-5bbaca56e59a" "answer-20e8f93c-cee8-5e58-b256-bc513601b9fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-430fd24f-3207-570e-9148-bac816a3c0be" :answer/id #uuid "430fd24f-3207-570e-9148-bac816a3c0be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1060863a-42e6-5905-845d-98d31c4eb37b" :answer/id #uuid "1060863a-42e6-5905-845d-98d31c4eb37b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-4y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29b072d1-5990-5d33-8af7-5bbaca56e59a" :answer/id #uuid "29b072d1-5990-5d33-8af7-5bbaca56e59a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=1-x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20e8f93c-cee8-5e58-b256-bc513601b9fd" :answer/id #uuid "20e8f93c-cee8-5e58-b256-bc513601b9fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=y-x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180033 :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177867 :answer-field/correct "answer-f53b17eb-0f4e-5b3d-ad45-ea23e6a6bb72" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbeedaa1-96af-5558-afe2-43dff6b5725b" "answer-1c26d151-cb3a-594e-8720-a6c407ef320d" "answer-03c4eae9-5741-51cc-ae04-ffcd3dbf9ba8" "answer-f53b17eb-0f4e-5b3d-ad45-ea23e6a6bb72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbeedaa1-96af-5558-afe2-43dff6b5725b" :answer/id #uuid "cbeedaa1-96af-5558-afe2-43dff6b5725b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=2xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c26d151-cb3a-594e-8720-a6c407ef320d" :answer/id #uuid "1c26d151-cb3a-594e-8720-a6c407ef320d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03c4eae9-5741-51cc-ae04-ffcd3dbf9ba8" :answer/id #uuid "03c4eae9-5741-51cc-ae04-ffcd3dbf9ba8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f53b17eb-0f4e-5b3d-ad45-ea23e6a6bb72" :answer/id #uuid "f53b17eb-0f4e-5b3d-ad45-ea23e6a6bb72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03021383-b99c-5e38-823a-268b5d1215a6" :question/id #uuid "03021383-b99c-5e38-823a-268b5d1215a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162160" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}})i+(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}})j$ over the domain $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}})i+(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}})j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}+(-\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}})=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/ed/edc12146ffad4dc46ed6d57b3dfdc9a08ca92fb601ef5e08ed9ccef3a1c1da4c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b9111900-5b3b-528e-bee1-da24812f7bc8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b9111900-5b3b-528e-bee1-da24812f7bc8" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9111900-5b3b-528e-bee1-da24812f7bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4f3ef020-6dd5-5ced-a63e-ffae9ca42093" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d25de580-d351-551b-9837-3c4daaa24a24" "answer-405b937b-2494-589a-941f-c2c89bb59b81" "answer-87b8b871-e4f1-5444-8738-aa304484251c" "answer-4f3ef020-6dd5-5ced-a63e-ffae9ca42093" "answer-595622b7-555e-511d-b8fb-61467363fd56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d25de580-d351-551b-9837-3c4daaa24a24" :answer/id #uuid "d25de580-d351-551b-9837-3c4daaa24a24" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1c/1c0270805f90797c9add194054ea5bbef76207b5ce9c9659227c069e999369f3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-405b937b-2494-589a-941f-c2c89bb59b81" :answer/id #uuid "405b937b-2494-589a-941f-c2c89bb59b81" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/44/4459ec44cd710a0ba13e9d22796311cb251cc58f8e41c4212a00260e45bbcd2c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87b8b871-e4f1-5444-8738-aa304484251c" :answer/id #uuid "87b8b871-e4f1-5444-8738-aa304484251c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/57/5725e9ce0bd4f2995d478f2a74d7b65f778157058a41ef0229637b40bb865b11.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f3ef020-6dd5-5ced-a63e-ffae9ca42093" :answer/id #uuid "4f3ef020-6dd5-5ced-a63e-ffae9ca42093" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a9/a9b8e953a6d1277056331aa46a3d3a6bf59ae0dd8dad66d14c8a5f2a409316ce.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-595622b7-555e-511d-b8fb-61467363fd56" :answer/id #uuid "595622b7-555e-511d-b8fb-61467363fd56" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b8be3b86eb85760320298c272e31dd76fa2fea5fd6acf2931831f1c0e3d73787.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180726 :tutorial/content "Let $\\psi(x,y)$ be a two-variable function defined on a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ We know that for a point $(x_0,y_0)\\in D,$ the vector\n\n$$\n\\left.\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}\n$$\n\nis *tangential* to the level curve $\\psi(x,y) = \\psi(x_0,y_0).$ \n\nBy computing this tangent vector at *every* point, we can generate a vector *field* tangential to the level curves of $\\psi$ at every point.\n\nWith that in mind, let \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n \n\nbe a vector field defined on $D.$ A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nTo construct a vector field with this stream function, we first compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j=2y\\,i-2x\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA diagram showing the surface $z = \\psi(x,y),$ its level curves, and the vector field $\\mathbf F$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/69/696e6c623dbc3ecfae2643a7317d3d176294624289ecf6efb16debdc947d0394.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ are circles of the form $x^2+y^2=c$, and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ in the $xy$-plane is tangential to these level curves.\n\n- Suppose $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity of a fluid in the $xy$-plane. Then, the level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ represent the path of a massless particle in the $xy$-plane moving with the flow. For this reason, the level curves of $\\psi$ are called **streamlines.**\n\n- The stream function of a vector field is typically not unique since we can add any constant to $\\psi$ to generate another valid stream function." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180763 :tutorial/content "Not all vector fields have an associated stream function! So, it's useful to have some way of determining whether a given vector field has a stream function or not. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field defined in a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ must be zero everywhere for a stream function to exist. \n\nWe won't give a detailed proof of this result here, but it's quite easy to see why it may be true. \n\nRecall that if $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the divergence of this vector field, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂y∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180759 :tutorial/content "Let's find a stream function for the following vector field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + e^x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nNote that our vector field $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ has the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad\nQ(x,y) = e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nLet's start by checking that a stream function exists. Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function since $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nTo find a stream function, we **partially integrate** these two equations and compare the results:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $y$ (that is, treating $x$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+f(x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is an arbitrary function.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $x$ (that is, treating $y$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+g(y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is an arbitrary function.\n\nComparing our two expressions for $\\psi,$ we get equality by setting $f=g=0.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = -e^x \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFinally, remember that the streamlines of $\\mathbf F$ are given by the level curves of $\\psi.$ So, to find the streamlines of $\\mathbf F,$ we plot the curves $\\psi = k$ for different values of $k.$ The level curves of $\\psi$ are always tangential to $\\mathbf F.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179868 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054693 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6e011359-1ec4-59a5-9ee5-39dc3634d579" "question-04603f4d-d37d-5464-90af-2ef30e760b2c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054601 :question/problem "Which of the given points is a solution to $y=-2x+1?$\n\n![](images/b9/b921dae09304534b2176dbc7a74c75c4ab36c76c67edb6b97cbe358bc703c6b5.png)" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1, 3)$ and $B(2,-4).$ To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $A(-1,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+1 \\\\ (3) & =-2(-1)+1 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $A(-1,3)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $B(2,-4)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+1 \\\\ (-4) & =-2(2)+1 \\\\ -4 & =-4+1 \\\\ -4 & =-3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $B(2,-4)$ is *not* solution to the equation.\n\nTherefore, only $A(-1,3)$ is a solution to the equation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054692 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b330576-64ef-57a2-934f-fa47fcfddd05" "question-c3d050d6-291f-571b-896c-061ed6a76fc9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054600 :question/problem "A solution to the linear equation $y-5x=2$ is the ordered pair $(2,k).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,k)$ is a solution to the linear equation $y-5x=2,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $2$ and $k$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5x & =2 \\\\ (k)-5(2) & =2 \\\\ k-10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-10 & =2 \\\\ k & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e011359-1ec4-59a5-9ee5-39dc3634d579" :question/id #uuid "6e011359-1ec4-59a5-9ee5-39dc3634d579" :question/math-academy-id "q-44433" :question/problem "Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to $y=-\\frac{x}{6}?$\n\nI. $(-18,-3)$\n\nII. $(6,1)$\n\nIII. $(12,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(-18,-3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ (-3) & =-\\frac{(-18)}{6} \\\\ -3 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(-18,-3)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(6,1)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ (1) & =-\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ 1 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a false statement, so $(6,1)$ is also *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(12,-2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ (-2) & =-\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(12,-2)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is a solution to the equation is III $(12,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be452970-7789-5eff-8788-2f246dfcea73"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be452970-7789-5eff-8788-2f246dfcea73" :answer-field/id #uuid "be452970-7789-5eff-8788-2f246dfcea73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a1f71324-2f7e-573d-ad3a-2c9b16b1da3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56338ef8-5dae-5fe2-b44c-d6d6be5007f0" "answer-88c554ae-2efb-5db6-a9c9-65801f7efdb6" "answer-a799586f-31b9-5a05-a6cd-524dc32c8bf1" "answer-21bd3c5d-172a-5398-96d2-3281455e0c23" "answer-a1f71324-2f7e-573d-ad3a-2c9b16b1da3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56338ef8-5dae-5fe2-b44c-d6d6be5007f0" :answer/id #uuid "56338ef8-5dae-5fe2-b44c-d6d6be5007f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88c554ae-2efb-5db6-a9c9-65801f7efdb6" :answer/id #uuid "88c554ae-2efb-5db6-a9c9-65801f7efdb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a799586f-31b9-5a05-a6cd-524dc32c8bf1" :answer/id #uuid "a799586f-31b9-5a05-a6cd-524dc32c8bf1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21bd3c5d-172a-5398-96d2-3281455e0c23" :answer/id #uuid "21bd3c5d-172a-5398-96d2-3281455e0c23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1f71324-2f7e-573d-ad3a-2c9b16b1da3a" :answer/id #uuid "a1f71324-2f7e-573d-ad3a-2c9b16b1da3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04603f4d-d37d-5464-90af-2ef30e760b2c" :question/id #uuid "04603f4d-d37d-5464-90af-2ef30e760b2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71678" :question/problem "Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to $y=2x-3?$\n\nI. $(-2,-7)$\n\nII. $(0,3)$\n\nIII. $(2,4)$" :question/worked-solution "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(-2,-7)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (-7) & =2(-2)-3 \\\\ -7 & =-4-3 \\\\ -7 & =-7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(-2,-7)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(0,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (3) & =2(0)-3 \\\\ 3 & =-3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(0,3)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(2,4)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (4) & =2(2)-3 \\\\ 4 & =4-3 \\\\ 4 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a false statement, so $(2,4)$ is also *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is a solution to the equation is I $(-2,-7).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4739ce09-2032-5914-8c63-7e80cf023353"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4739ce09-2032-5914-8c63-7e80cf023353" :answer-field/id #uuid "4739ce09-2032-5914-8c63-7e80cf023353" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-702e74e5-9f57-53de-953b-d80198dd18ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6a5f06f-62e9-5982-84f3-513bfda69851" "answer-702e74e5-9f57-53de-953b-d80198dd18ba" "answer-44e97b6c-2103-5102-9faa-5aca41fc66f0" "answer-9886ec6c-45f3-5247-ade4-767bba35097f" "answer-fe65337d-52f9-51bc-9db9-c7c11a12af42"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6a5f06f-62e9-5982-84f3-513bfda69851" :answer/id #uuid "e6a5f06f-62e9-5982-84f3-513bfda69851" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-702e74e5-9f57-53de-953b-d80198dd18ba" :answer/id #uuid "702e74e5-9f57-53de-953b-d80198dd18ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44e97b6c-2103-5102-9faa-5aca41fc66f0" :answer/id #uuid "44e97b6c-2103-5102-9faa-5aca41fc66f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9886ec6c-45f3-5247-ade4-767bba35097f" :answer/id #uuid "9886ec6c-45f3-5247-ade4-767bba35097f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe65337d-52f9-51bc-9db9-c7c11a12af42" :answer/id #uuid "fe65337d-52f9-51bc-9db9-c7c11a12af42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b330576-64ef-57a2-934f-fa47fcfddd05" :question/id #uuid "2b330576-64ef-57a2-934f-fa47fcfddd05" :question/math-academy-id "q-44421" :question/problem "A solution to the linear equation $3x-4y=2$ is the ordered pair $(-2,k).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(-2,k)$ is a solution to the linear equation $3x-4y=2,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $-2$ and $k$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $-2$ and $y$ with $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-4y & =2 \\\\ 3(-2)-4(k) & =2 \\\\ -6-4k & =2 \\\\ -4k & =8 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9856d1a4-8c16-5b8c-a1d3-d2714ffaf3a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9856d1a4-8c16-5b8c-a1d3-d2714ffaf3a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9856d1a4-8c16-5b8c-a1d3-d2714ffaf3a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e28cbb48-ea6e-54a8-858c-bd88e8434742" :answer-field/choices ["answer-53db5663-0069-566e-9fc8-115a3b399587" "answer-e28cbb48-ea6e-54a8-858c-bd88e8434742" "answer-cd7e07b4-1c10-5f17-80e6-3214dee79832" "answer-58e11586-8d67-518d-a843-9027adadcf30" "answer-b33cb1ad-4e00-521b-87c0-9aa857e66b3d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53db5663-0069-566e-9fc8-115a3b399587" :answer/id #uuid "53db5663-0069-566e-9fc8-115a3b399587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e28cbb48-ea6e-54a8-858c-bd88e8434742" :answer/id #uuid "e28cbb48-ea6e-54a8-858c-bd88e8434742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd7e07b4-1c10-5f17-80e6-3214dee79832" :answer/id #uuid "cd7e07b4-1c10-5f17-80e6-3214dee79832" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58e11586-8d67-518d-a843-9027adadcf30" :answer/id #uuid "58e11586-8d67-518d-a843-9027adadcf30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b33cb1ad-4e00-521b-87c0-9aa857e66b3d" :answer/id #uuid "b33cb1ad-4e00-521b-87c0-9aa857e66b3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3d050d6-291f-571b-896c-061ed6a76fc9" :question/id #uuid "c3d050d6-291f-571b-896c-061ed6a76fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109037" :question/problem "A solution to the linear equation $y=4x-11$ is the ordered pair $(2,h).$ What is the value of $h?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,h)$ is a solution to the linear equation $y=4x-11,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $2$ and $h$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $h,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x-11 \\\\ (h) & =4(2)-11 \\\\ h & =8-11 \\\\ h & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2df45d58-6a1d-5cd7-baed-6c5a3a222eac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2df45d58-6a1d-5cd7-baed-6c5a3a222eac" :answer-field/id #uuid "2df45d58-6a1d-5cd7-baed-6c5a3a222eac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-067c44d3-8150-574c-a44d-e208db72c5ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cce19abc-3226-57b7-802f-3d71ca5373e1" "answer-067c44d3-8150-574c-a44d-e208db72c5ee" "answer-0cf455f0-1f2c-5ba3-aa35-ee30c16a38d5" "answer-b5e0a9bf-0018-5d22-b9fb-a05f84f821e1" "answer-d9217400-cdfc-5a2e-97b5-b0d1c59e8d48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cce19abc-3226-57b7-802f-3d71ca5373e1" :answer/id #uuid "cce19abc-3226-57b7-802f-3d71ca5373e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-067c44d3-8150-574c-a44d-e208db72c5ee" :answer/id #uuid "067c44d3-8150-574c-a44d-e208db72c5ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cf455f0-1f2c-5ba3-aa35-ee30c16a38d5" :answer/id #uuid "0cf455f0-1f2c-5ba3-aa35-ee30c16a38d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5e0a9bf-0018-5d22-b9fb-a05f84f821e1" :answer/id #uuid "b5e0a9bf-0018-5d22-b9fb-a05f84f821e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9217400-cdfc-5a2e-97b5-b0d1c59e8d48" :answer/id #uuid "d9217400-cdfc-5a2e-97b5-b0d1c59e8d48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055944 :tutorial/content "A **linear equation** in the variables $x$ and $y$ is an equation that involves just these two variables, possibly with some constants. There should be no powers on any of the variables and the variables can't be multiplied together.\n\nFor example, the following are linear equations:\n\n$$\ny = x + 4, \\qquad\n\ty = -3x, \\qquad\n\t5y = 6x,\\qquad\n\t2x + 3y = 9\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the following are *not* linear equations:\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 2x, \\qquad\n\ty^2 = - 4x + 2, \\qquad\n\ty = 2xy\n$$\n\nA **solution** of a linear equation is an **ordered pair** $(x,y)$ that makes the equation true. \n\nFor instance, the ordered pair $(3, 7)$ is a solution to the equation $y = 3x - 2$ because if we replace $x$ with $3$ and $y$ with $7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-2 \\\\ (7) & =3(3)-2 \\\\ 7 & =9-2 \\\\ 7 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is a true statement, so $(3, 7)$ is a solution to the equation $y = 3x - 2.$\n\n**Important**: A linear equation in two variables has an infinite number of solutions, not just one!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054934 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186160486 :answer-field/choices 17592186160487] [:db/retract 17592186160486 :answer-field/choices 17592186160488] [:db/retract 17592186160486 :answer-field/choices 17592186160489] [:db/retract 17592186160486 :answer-field/choices 17592186160490] [:db/retract 17592186160486 :answer-field/choices 17592186160491] {:db/id 17592186162156 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0ecf28e6-85ea-58f8-8358-bdfe911fb50e" "question-332a6eec-904f-5523-bf1a-c62328e3b046"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162157 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-64fea66a-24ac-5ed4-9315-868a66adf40d" "question-7fdea743-d7bb-5faa-abc9-1bdf19e0dbae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162158 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2472238f-de89-5651-9808-c29025aa1081"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ecf28e6-85ea-58f8-8358-bdfe911fb50e" :question/id #uuid "0ecf28e6-85ea-58f8-8358-bdfe911fb50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-253542" :question/problem "What is the infimum of the set $C=\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}?$\n\n$inf(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $C$ is the greatest lower bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the infimum of $C$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(C) & =min\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-641a7bf6-8d64-5bbd-b720-80aba2e3fd36"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-641a7bf6-8d64-5bbd-b720-80aba2e3fd36" :answer-field/id #uuid "641a7bf6-8d64-5bbd-b720-80aba2e3fd36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b3db2d41-9f95-5454-99e3-3731cf3c7216" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3db2d41-9f95-5454-99e3-3731cf3c7216"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3db2d41-9f95-5454-99e3-3731cf3c7216" :answer/id #uuid "b3db2d41-9f95-5454-99e3-3731cf3c7216" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-332a6eec-904f-5523-bf1a-c62328e3b046" :question/id #uuid "332a6eec-904f-5523-bf1a-c62328e3b046" :question/math-academy-id "q-253552" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot n:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{1+1}\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot 3 & =3 \\\\ (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot 4 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-383aa34f-a1d1-5f56-a588-24ec2c1c5712"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-383aa34f-a1d1-5f56-a588-24ec2c1c5712" :answer-field/id #uuid "383aa34f-a1d1-5f56-a588-24ec2c1c5712" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3dcae1ab-8b52-5672-bb43-dd85e7593b29" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3dcae1ab-8b52-5672-bb43-dd85e7593b29"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dcae1ab-8b52-5672-bb43-dd85e7593b29" :answer/id #uuid "3dcae1ab-8b52-5672-bb43-dd85e7593b29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64fea66a-24ac-5ed4-9315-868a66adf40d" :question/id #uuid "64fea66a-24ac-5ed4-9315-868a66adf40d" :question/math-academy-id "q-253555" :question/problem "Consider the set $A=[1,\\sqrt{5}].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{5}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{5}$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min\\,[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{5}$ is also the maximum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-419f4350-8ea2-5ca7-9094-f580a6bac952" "answer-field-149b46b7-83ac-5b5a-b14c-9564fbc3ed3d" "answer-field-b76bb8b2-2c1f-5117-8531-fe1007b02a61" "answer-field-4e941132-5627-5132-94a5-af608b2e9c1e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-419f4350-8ea2-5ca7-9094-f580a6bac952" :answer-field/id #uuid "419f4350-8ea2-5ca7-9094-f580a6bac952" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2653f489-1ea3-5e38-8c0d-93a677463b87" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fac0bcdb-7722-530d-a229-b7328db97990" "answer-b110ac52-2731-52a3-841d-939f0817a22b" "answer-71e73e17-bbbf-5739-a748-e8f1e08c6fae" "answer-2653f489-1ea3-5e38-8c0d-93a677463b87"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fac0bcdb-7722-530d-a229-b7328db97990" :answer/id #uuid "fac0bcdb-7722-530d-a229-b7328db97990" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "greatest lower bound" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b110ac52-2731-52a3-841d-939f0817a22b" :answer/id #uuid "b110ac52-2731-52a3-841d-939f0817a22b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "greatest upper bound" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71e73e17-bbbf-5739-a748-e8f1e08c6fae" :answer/id #uuid "71e73e17-bbbf-5739-a748-e8f1e08c6fae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "smallest lower bound" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2653f489-1ea3-5e38-8c0d-93a677463b87" :answer/id #uuid "2653f489-1ea3-5e38-8c0d-93a677463b87" :answer/type :ans