020fc-4a55-5fba-8584-f1036867b70b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7fe83c22-4de8-5c95-887f-8324f25e6fb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7fe83c22-4de8-5c95-887f-8324f25e6fb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fe83c22-4de8-5c95-887f-8324f25e6fb6" :answer/id #uuid "7fe83c22-4de8-5c95-887f-8324f25e6fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72f1e9f4-dfe2-5157-9a7e-252993718bb0" :question/id #uuid "72f1e9f4-dfe2-5157-9a7e-252993718bb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-44453" :question/problem "A horizontal line and a vertical line meet at the point $(1,4).$ What are the equations of the vertical and horizontal lines, respectively?" :question/worked-solution "Since the vertical line should pass through $(1,4)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $1.$ Hence, the equation of that line is $x=1.$\n\nSince the horizontal line should pass through $(1,4)$, the $y$-value for every point on the line is $4.$ Hence, the equation of that line is $y=4.$\n\n![](images/16/16e33da178489ccfc16b3e4cef5632d95df18b50ece5ae859f666120827da929.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50ef4e91-7804-5a4a-b126-9e76f4a526f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50ef4e91-7804-5a4a-b126-9e76f4a526f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "50ef4e91-7804-5a4a-b126-9e76f4a526f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d095737e-6521-55d3-a8e6-bb35da8ccbde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b83ea7a-7427-5328-ba07-11fb3545b16e" "answer-fd4a7f28-4e44-53ab-85e3-a4f8034dc5e2" "answer-e3e616a6-03c2-550d-bf2c-e11dc888fd14" "answer-d095737e-6521-55d3-a8e6-bb35da8ccbde" "answer-e9f46ae0-be31-589f-943a-84782d3ee639"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b83ea7a-7427-5328-ba07-11fb3545b16e" :answer/id #uuid "0b83ea7a-7427-5328-ba07-11fb3545b16e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$, $y=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd4a7f28-4e44-53ab-85e3-a4f8034dc5e2" :answer/id #uuid "fd4a7f28-4e44-53ab-85e3-a4f8034dc5e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-4$, $y=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3e616a6-03c2-550d-bf2c-e11dc888fd14" :answer/id #uuid "e3e616a6-03c2-550d-bf2c-e11dc888fd14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$, $y=-4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d095737e-6521-55d3-a8e6-bb35da8ccbde" :answer/id #uuid "d095737e-6521-55d3-a8e6-bb35da8ccbde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$, $y=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9f46ae0-be31-589f-943a-84782d3ee639" :answer/id #uuid "e9f46ae0-be31-589f-943a-84782d3ee639" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=4$, $y=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056014 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the equation of the **horizontal line** that passes through the point $(0,3)$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/37/379af2ba930fa537f1fbdda5b4b5cc91e355a46e90918cdc56bbce66f3502d7b.png)\n\nNotice that for *any* $x$-value on the line, the $y$-value is always $\\color{red}3.$ \n\n![](images/ff/ff5ec246a558fc981ba5f0b0085ce3663ac6ab837121f31a5d44de6adb91cb90.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=3.$\n\n**Note**: When writing the equation of a horizontal line, we always put the variable $y$ on the left side and the constant on the right side." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056015 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to find the equation of the **vertical line** that passes through the point $(-4,0)$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/06/065dfc149c1624bbe2c6fd51085c48b5e60c1d37d248d073dd1374d08e65b5af.png)\n\nNotice that for *any* $y$-value on the line, the $x$-value is always $\\color{red}-4.$ \n\n![](images/01/01d24274cc3d2b5a7899cc1aa8cee4aef4fb02ddb45acd27629a57d6af41a398.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of this vertical line is $x=-4.$\n\n**Note**: When writing the equation of a vertical line, we always put the variable $x$ on the left side and the constant on the right side." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054937 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083180 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ea4d21a-4360-5351-8a08-59d8247080d9" "question-aa05eb09-9ab6-5ac2-b2dd-bab3e1656e0c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ea4d21a-4360-5351-8a08-59d8247080d9" :question/id #uuid "9ea4d21a-4360-5351-8a08-59d8247080d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-6844" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c12cd35e66acc00de926a2375460242e46f359548a5112f0d87654485ba9a42.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,0).$ However note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(3,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $4,$ including $4$ but not including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0<y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as  $0<f(x)\\le 4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb48cbc4-341a-5740-83c8-b736e21b49ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb48cbc4-341a-5740-83c8-b736e21b49ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb48cbc4-341a-5740-83c8-b736e21b49ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b4f4007-9614-5c27-86dc-5b6c8f4e5221" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c9e33fa-f145-5c62-8aba-4b06a341ec87" "answer-88bd4100-5b4f-5acb-9ca3-94b61db3e012" "answer-1b4f4007-9614-5c27-86dc-5b6c8f4e5221" "answer-b49f81b1-bee6-52ee-8ec8-c7969c90282b" "answer-9143cb9d-1e7b-5d6c-826e-9e30878041d6"] :db/ensur